::CW_U03_L07_Q01::Scene plan: Theme: belonging requires invitation. Conflict: a student is excluded from a lunch table. Detail: one character moves a backpack to make room. What makes the plan integrated?{
~A. The backpack is unrelated to the scene.#Incorrect. It creates space for belonging.
=B. The conflict and detail both support belonging.#Correct. The table action develops the central idea.
~C. The theme should be removed.#Incorrect. It guides the scene.
~D. Exclusion prevents all fiction development.#Incorrect. Conflict can develop fiction.
}
::CW_U03_L07_Q02::Passage: Imani thinks winning means standing alone. Her mentor gives her a list of names before the final project and says, "No one builds a bridge by holding one rope." What source pattern is most likely adapted?{
~A. A random recipe.#Incorrect. No cooking pattern appears.
~B. A conflict with no source influence.#Incorrect. The guide-object pattern is clear.
~C. A copied original plot.#Incorrect. The source element is transformed.
=D. A guide gives an object before a difficult journey.#Correct. The mentor and list function as guide and object.
}
::CW_U03_L07_Q03::Central idea: responsibility is shared. Which character change best supports it?{
=A. The character moves from controlling every task to trusting teammates with important work.#Correct. The change directly supports shared responsibility.
~B. The character refuses all help forever.#Incorrect. That works against the idea.
~C. The character changes shoes.#Incorrect. This is unrelated by itself.
~D. The character forgets the project.#Incorrect. That does not develop shared responsibility.
}
::CW_U03_L07_Q04::A scene adapts a warning-before-risk pattern. The character ignores a lab partner's warning and launches the project early. What theme can this support?{
~A. Warnings have no purpose.#Incorrect. The warning matters.
~B. Labs cannot be adapted settings.#Incorrect. Modern settings can adapt source patterns.
=C. Pride can make a person ignore useful guidance.#Correct. The warning and risky choice develop pride.
~D. Risk always leads to success.#Incorrect. The theme is about danger of pride.
}
::CW_U03_L07_Q05::Which plan best integrates author purpose with central idea?{
=A. Purpose: make readers question independence; central idea: accepting help can show maturity; detail: character finally hands a box to a friend.#Correct. The purpose, idea, and action align.
~B. Purpose: show kindness; central idea: weather is cold; detail: a random clock.#Incorrect. The pieces do not work together.
~C. Purpose: create humor; central idea: honesty matters; detail: unrelated shoes.#Incorrect. The detail does not support the idea.
~D. Purpose: no reader effect; central idea: none; detail: everything.#Incorrect. Integrated fiction needs focus.
}
::CW_U03_L07_Q06::Passage: A character keeps an old recipe card while moving to a new city and later cooks from it for a lonely neighbor. Which skills are integrated?{
~A. Only punctuation.#Incorrect. The passage develops fiction meaning.
~B. A source with no transformation.#Incorrect. The recipe can function as a guide-object influence.
~C. No central idea.#Incorrect. The details suggest memory and belonging.
=D. Theme, meaningful object, and character action.#Correct. The recipe card supports memory and connection through action.
}
::CW_U03_L07_Q07::A student's fiction plan includes a source object that never appears after the first sentence. What is the best revision?{
~A. Add five more unused objects.#Incorrect. More unused details weaken focus.
=B. Make the object affect the conflict or character change.#Correct. Integrated details should matter.
~C. Remove the character growth.#Incorrect. Growth is central.
~D. Keep it because all objects are automatically symbolic.#Incorrect. Objects need purpose.
}
::CW_U03_L07_Q08::Scene plan: Starting belief: asking for help means failure. Conflicting perspective: a coach says revision is part of excellence. Changed understanding: the character asks for critique. What kind of arc is this?{
~A. A setting list only.#Incorrect. It focuses on belief and change.
~B. A theme with no character.#Incorrect. A character is central.
=C. Coming-of-age growth arc.#Correct. The character matures through changed understanding.
~D. A copied source.#Incorrect. It is a growth arc.
}
::CW_U03_L07_Q09::Which detail best supports a theme about honesty repairing harm?{
~A. The character changes socks.#Incorrect. Unrelated.
~B. The character ignores the person harmed.#Incorrect. That does not repair harm.
~C. The character describes the ceiling only.#Incorrect. Not connected to honesty.
=D. The character returns the hidden letter and apologizes directly.#Correct. The action repairs secrecy.
}
::CW_U03_L07_Q10::Passage: A narrator thinks family stories waste time until an old notebook proves the streets in the stories were real. What reader effect does the notebook create?{
=A. It makes the narrator's changed understanding believable.#Correct. The notebook provides evidence for the shift.
~B. It removes family history.#Incorrect. It reveals history.
~C. It makes the stories pointless.#Incorrect. It proves their value.
~D. It creates no effect.#Incorrect. It changes interpretation.
}
::CW_U03_L07_Q11::What is the strongest integrated fiction planning sequence?{
~A. Random object, random setting, random dialogue, no purpose.#Incorrect. This lacks integration.
~B. Ending first, then remove all evidence.#Incorrect. Evidence is needed.
=C. Central idea, character belief, conflict, source/detail choices, changed understanding.#Correct. This sequence connects purpose and development.
~D. Theme only with no scene.#Incorrect. Fiction needs development.
}
::CW_U03_L07_Q12::Passage: The map from a teammate appears when the leader gets lost and finally asks the team to choose the route together. What does the map do?{
~A. It distracts because maps can never matter.#Incorrect. It matters directly.
=B. It supports the theme that leadership can require shared guidance.#Correct. The object affects conflict and growth.
~C. It proves the leader should work alone.#Incorrect. It leads to shared choice.
~D. It removes the team.#Incorrect. The team becomes important.
}
::CW_U03_L07_Q13::A plan compares two themes: one passage shows courage through speaking up; another shows courage through leaving an unsafe place. What should the comparison explain?{
=A. How both develop courage differently through different actions.#Correct. It compares shared theme and development.
~B. Only that both have characters.#Incorrect. That is too broad.
~C. That the passages are identical.#Incorrect. They develop courage differently.
~D. That courage has no evidence.#Incorrect. Evidence is required.
}
::CW_U03_L07_Q14::Which source influence best supports a character learning humility?{
~A. A random lunch with no conflict.#Incorrect. It does not support the arc.
~B. A copied ending with no change.#Incorrect. Transformation and growth are missing.
~C. A weather report only.#Incorrect. Weather alone does not support humility.
=D. A pride-before-fall pattern transformed into a student ignoring advice before a performance.#Correct. Pride and consequence support humility.
}
::CW_U03_L07_Q15::Passage: The narrator repeats "I had it under control" before each mistake. What purpose does the repetition serve?{
~A. It proves control is perfect.#Incorrect. Mistakes contradict that.
~B. It removes character perspective.#Incorrect. It reveals perspective.
=C. It reveals the narrator's blind spot about control.#Correct. Repetition exposes self-deception.
~D. It explains grammar.#Incorrect. It is craft purpose.
}
::CW_U03_L07_Q16::Central idea: memory can guide a person forward. Which adapted object fits best?{
~A. An unrelated parking ticket.#Incorrect. It does not connect by itself.
=B. A grandmother's recipe card used in a new city.#Correct. The object carries memory into new action.
~C. A random pencil with no role.#Incorrect. It lacks meaning.
~D. A copied magical object with no transformation.#Incorrect. Transformation is needed.
}
::CW_U03_L07_Q17::A student's scene has theme, conflict, and growth, but no evidence in the explanation. What should be added?{
~A. More vague praise.#Incorrect. Vague praise is not evidence.
~B. A completely unrelated summary.#Incorrect. It does not support the explanation.
~C. No details at all.#Incorrect. Evidence is required.
=D. Specific passage details showing how the elements develop the idea.#Correct. Evidence supports analysis.
}
::CW_U03_L07_Q18::Passage: The old key no longer opens the house, but the character keeps it while choosing a new apartment. What central idea might it support?{
=A. Leaving can include carrying memory forward.#Correct. The key connects past and change.
~B. Keys are useless in every story.#Incorrect. The key has symbolic value.
~C. Apartments cannot have themes.#Incorrect. Setting can support theme.
~D. Memory prevents all change.#Incorrect. The character still chooses a new apartment.
}
::CW_U03_L07_Q19::Which plan best shows conflicting perspectives?{
~A. Both characters agree without tension.#Incorrect. No conflict.
=B. One character sees moving as betrayal, while another sees it as survival.#Correct. The same choice has different meanings.
~C. A character names every object in a room.#Incorrect. Listing is not perspective conflict.
~D. No character has an opinion.#Incorrect. Perspective requires viewpoint.
}
::CW_U03_L07_Q20::What makes an adaptation purposeful in integrated fiction?{
~A. It changes only font.#Incorrect. Font is not enough.
~B. It copies everything.#Incorrect. Adaptation transforms.
=C. The transformed source element affects theme, conflict, or character growth.#Correct. It must matter to the scene.
~D. It has no reason.#Incorrect. Purpose is required.
}
::CW_U03_L07_Q21::Scene plan: A character wants independence, receives a mentor's marked-up draft, resists feedback, then revises successfully. What theme is strongest?{
=A. Growth can require accepting guidance.#Correct. The mentor object and revision arc support the theme.
~B. Feedback always ruins writing.#Incorrect. Feedback helps.
~C. Independence means ignoring everyone.#Incorrect. The character changes from that belief.
~D. Drafts have no meaning.#Incorrect. The draft is central.
}
::CW_U03_L07_Q22::Which response best explains integration?{
~A. The story uses many names.#Incorrect. Names alone do not integrate meaning.
~B. The writer avoids revision.#Incorrect. Integration often requires revision.
=C. The source object, central idea, and character change all point to the same reader effect.#Correct. This defines integrated fiction.
~D. The setting is long.#Incorrect. Length does not prove integration.
}
::CW_U03_L07_Q23::Passage: A character says courage means never fearing anything, then speaks while her hands shake. What changed understanding appears?{
~A. Courage means silence.#Incorrect. She speaks.
~B. Fear stops all action.#Incorrect. She acts.
~C. Hands are the theme.#Incorrect. Shaking hands are evidence.
=D. Courage can exist while fear is still present.#Correct. The action revises her belief.
}
::CW_U03_L07_Q24::A scene has a strong central idea but an unrelated comic side plot that takes half the scene. What revision is best?{
~A. Add more unrelated comedy.#Incorrect. This worsens the issue.
=B. Cut or revise the side plot so it supports the central idea.#Correct. Integration requires focus.
~C. Remove the central idea.#Incorrect. It guides the scene.
~D. Keep everything because length equals quality.#Incorrect. Length is not integration.
}
::CW_U03_L07_Q25::Which final check best prepares an integrated fiction scene?{
=A. Do theme, source influence, central idea, purpose, conflict, and growth support the same effect?#Correct. This checks Unit 3 integration.
~B. Did I add details randomly?#Incorrect. Random details weaken integration.
~C. Did I remove all character change?#Incorrect. Character growth matters.
~D. Did I ignore evidence?#Incorrect. Evidence is required.
}
